WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.000
If you rely on benefit programs like SSI or Medicaid, those monthly checks and the health coverage that comes with them, you know the drill.

00:09.000 --> 00:17.000
Every dollar that comes into your life has to be carefully tracked, and holding on to a little extra cash can feel incredibly risky.

00:17.000 --> 00:22.000
For decades, the system has enforced a strict $2,000 asset limit.

00:22.000 --> 00:25.000
Think of this as an invisible ceiling.

00:25.000 --> 00:33.000
If you earn wages, receive a gift from a relative, or try to build an emergency fund, you hit that ceiling immediately.

00:33.000 --> 00:36.000
Crossing that line triggers severe penalties.

00:36.000 --> 00:41.000
Your health care and your income support are immediately suspended or lost completely.

00:41.000 --> 00:44.000
There is a legal way around this restriction.

00:44.000 --> 00:48.000
It's called an ABLE account, sometimes known as a 529A.

00:48.000 --> 00:56.000
This is a special account where your money can grow without being taxed, acting as a secure bypass for your savings.

00:56.000 --> 01:00.000
When you use this account, accumulating money stops being a threat to your benefits.

01:00.000 --> 01:06.000
You can finally save for what you need, while keeping your support system in place.

01:06.000 --> 01:10.000
This animated diagram shows exactly how the protection works.

01:10.000 --> 01:16.000
Money placed inside an ABLE account is mostly invisible to the government's benefit offices.

01:16.000 --> 01:22.000
You can save up to $100,000 in this account, before your SSI cash benefits are even paused.

01:22.000 --> 01:25.000
And your health care is even more secure.

01:25.000 --> 01:32.000
Even if your account balance grows beyond that $100,000 mark, your Medicaid coverage remains fully intact.

01:32.000 --> 01:38.000
Originally, the rule stated you are eligible only if your disability began before your 26th birthday.

01:38.000 --> 01:41.000
This timeline shows a major change that's coming.

01:41.000 --> 01:48.000
Starting January 1, 2026, the age limit for when a disability starts jumps up to age 46.

01:48.000 --> 01:50.000
Proving you qualify is straightforward.

01:50.000 --> 01:59.000
If you already receive SSI, or SSDI, which is like an insurance program for people who have worked but can no longer do so, you are automatically eligible.

01:59.000 --> 02:03.000
Otherwise, you just need a simple letter from your doctor.

02:03.000 --> 02:07.000
This update recognizes that disability can happen at many different stages of life,

02:07.000 --> 02:12.000
and it opens up these protections to millions of older adults and veterans.

02:12.000 --> 02:16.000
An ABLE account is a team effort. Anyone can put money into it.

02:16.000 --> 02:19.000
Parents, friends, or you, the account owner.

02:19.000 --> 02:27.000
In this progress bar, the standard limit for all contributions combined is $20,000 per year, as of 2026.

02:27.000 --> 02:30.000
There is an extra rule for people who have a job and receive a paycheck.

02:30.000 --> 02:33.000
It's called the ABLE to work rule.

02:33.000 --> 02:37.000
If you are working, you can contribute thousands of extra dollars from your own income,

02:37.000 --> 02:41.000
pushing your total savings well past that standard $20,000 cap.

02:41.000 --> 02:46.000
This means that earning more money at your job won't force you back into a state of poverty.

02:46.000 --> 02:50.000
You can accept a raise or work extra hours while keeping your savings safe.

02:50.000 --> 02:55.000
When you start using your savings, the rules for what you can buy are very flexible.

02:55.000 --> 03:01.000
The tax office uses a specific term for these, qualified disability expenses,

03:01.000 --> 03:05.000
but the list of what's allowed is much longer than you might think.

03:05.000 --> 03:12.000
Many people assume these funds must be spent exclusively on medical bills, hospitals, or therapy.

03:12.000 --> 03:18.000
In reality, you can use your account for almost anything that helps you live a normal life.

03:18.000 --> 03:25.000
That includes your rent, transportation costs like a van or bus pass, a smartphone, or even your education.

03:25.000 --> 03:31.000
The official definition is broad, an expense is valid if it relates to your disability

03:31.000 --> 03:36.000
and helps improve your health, your independence, or your overall quality of life.

03:36.000 --> 03:39.000
These funds are meant for your everyday reality.

03:39.000 --> 03:46.000
They give you the flexibility to handle your own needs without having to ask for permission for every purchase.

03:46.000 --> 03:53.000
No matter who sets up the account or helps you manage it, the person with the disability is the absolute owner.

03:53.000 --> 03:57.000
For daily spending, many programs offer an ABLE debit card.

03:57.000 --> 04:03.000
You can tap it to pay for your everyday expenses directly from your account, just like any other bank card.

04:03.000 --> 04:09.000
While nearly every state runs its own ABLE program, you aren't restricted to your home state.

04:09.000 --> 04:13.000
You can shop online to compare different plans and choose the one with the lowest fees.

04:13.000 --> 04:16.000
Setting one up takes just minutes.

04:16.000 --> 04:21.000
The entire process is online, with no lawyers required and no complex paperwork.

04:21.000 --> 04:26.000
If you work and contribute your own money, you may also qualify for a savers credit.

04:26.000 --> 04:32.000
This is essentially a reward from the government in the form of a tax credit, just for saving your own income.

04:32.000 --> 04:35.000
An ABLE account is more than just a place to put money.

04:35.000 --> 04:41.000
It is a practical tool that gives you the power to save, spend, and thrive on your own terms.

